{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"display_name":"Python 3","language":"python","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[{"sourceType":"competition","sourceId":169382}],"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":5,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"id":"295b4c7b","cell_type":"markdown","source":"# OULAD regression: predicting a student's weighted assessment score\n\n**Task.** For every (student, module, presentation) predict `weighted_score` (0–100). Metric: **MSE** (lower is better).\n\n**Summary of my approach**\n1. Work out the *grain* of every table and check every merge (row counts before/after).\n2. EDA: target shape (bimodal, many drop-outs), demographics, engagement in the VLE.\n3. **Leakage decision.** The competition README states that `studentAssessment.csv` is *intentionally absent* (\"not a bug, but a feature\") because the organisers removed everything that can leak the target. The archive nevertheless contains it (without scores). The weight of the assessments a student *submitted* is almost a deterministic upper bound of the target, so I **do not use that file in the final model**. I only use it in one clearly labelled experiment (7.6) to measure how much it would have \"helped\", i.e. how large the leak is.\n4. Instead I engineer legitimate proxies from the VLE logs: activity profile over the course, activity types, **click bursts before each assessment deadline** (my own idea for \"did the student probably do the assessment\"), and cohort-relative percentiles.\n5. Hypotheses are tested with 5-fold CV in the format *observation → hypothesis → change → local metric → conclusion*; the final model is a CV-weighted blend.\n\n**Notebook map:** 1 Setup · 2 Tables & grain · 3 EDA · 4 Leakage discussion · 5 Features · 6 Validation · 7 Experiments · 8 Error analysis · 9 Final model & submission · 10 Conclusions.\n\n**Sources.** Dataset: Kuzilek J., Hlosta M., Zdrahal Z., *Open University Learning Analytics dataset*, Sci. Data 4:170171 (2017), doi:10.1038/sdata.2017.171; competition README (Kaggle). Code written by me using the pandas / scikit-learn / seaborn documentation; no external solutions were reused.","metadata":{}},{"id":"8eeef100","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 1. Setup","metadata":{}},{"id":"b7eadb88","cell_type":"code","source":"import os, time, sys, zipfile, warnings\nimport numpy as np, pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt, seaborn as sns\nfrom scipy.optimize import nnls\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import KFold, GroupKFold, train_test_split\nfrom sklearn.dummy import DummyRegressor\nfrom sklearn.ensemble import HistGradientBoostingRegressor, HistGradientBoostingClassifier, RandomForestRegressor\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import RidgeCV\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\nfrom sklearn.compose import ColumnTransformer, make_column_selector\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import OneHotEncoder, OrdinalEncoder, StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer\nfrom sklearn.metrics import mean_squared_error, mean_absolute_error, r2_score\n\nwarnings.filterwarnings('ignore')\nsns.set_theme(style='whitegrid', context='notebook')\nplt.rcParams.update({'figure.dpi': 100, 'axes.titlesize': 11, 'axes.labelsize': 10})\nSEED = 42\nQUICK = False                       # True = fast smoke test (fewer trees / iterations / folds)\nN_SPLITS = 3 if QUICK else 5\nRF_TREES = 10 if QUICK else 100\nITER_SCALE = 0.1 if QUICK else 1.0  # scales boosting iterations\nT0 = time.time()\ndef tic(msg=''):\n    print(f'[{time.time()-T0:6.0f}s] {msg}'); print(f'[{time.time()-T0:6.0f}s] {msg}', file=sys.__stderr__, flush=True)\n\nDATA_DIR = None\nfor cand in ['.', 'data', 'ur-fu-2026-reg-oulad', '/kaggle/input/ur-fu-2026-reg-oulad', '/home/claude/oulad']:\n    if os.path.exists(os.path.join(cand, 'Train_Who.csv')):\n        DATA_DIR = cand; break\nif DATA_DIR is None and os.path.exists('ur-fu-2026-reg-oulad.zip'):\n    zipfile.ZipFile('ur-fu-2026-reg-oulad.zip').extractall('data'); DATA_DIR = 'data'\nassert DATA_DIR is not None, 'Put the competition files (or the zip) next to the notebook'\nprint('data dir:', DATA_DIR, '| QUICK =', QUICK)","metadata":{},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["data dir: /home/claude/oulad | QUICK = False\n"]}],"execution_count":1},{"id":"4d7c27df","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 2. Tables and their grain\n\nBefore any merge I need to know *what one row means* in each table.","metadata":{}},{"id":"63039705","cell_type":"code","source":"rd = lambda f, **kw: pd.read_csv(os.path.join(DATA_DIR, f), **kw)\ntrain_who, test_who = rd('Train_Who.csv'), rd('Test_Who.csv')\ny_tr, sample_sub = rd('Train_Target_reg.csv'), rd('Submission_reg.csv')\nsi, sr, courses = rd('studentInfo.csv'), rd('studentRegistration.csv'), rd('courses.csv')\nassessments, vle = rd('assessments.csv'), rd('vle.csv')\nK = ['id_student', 'code_module', 'code_presentation']   # the student-in-a-presentation key\nP = ['code_module', 'code_presentation']\nfor name, df, keys in [('Train_Who', train_who, K), ('Test_Who', test_who, K), ('studentInfo', si, K), ('studentRegistration', sr, K),\n                       ('courses', courses, P), ('assessments', assessments, ['id_assessment']), ('vle', vle, ['id_site'])]:\n    print(f'{name:20s} rows={len(df):>8,}  duplicated rows on key {keys}: {df.duplicated(keys).sum():,}')\nprint('train/test overlap on key:', len(train_who.merge(test_who, on=K)))\nprint('test students that also occur in train (other presentation): %.1f%%' % (100 * test_who.id_student.isin(train_who.id_student).mean()))\nprint('same presentations in train and test:', sorted(train_who.code_presentation.unique()) == sorted(test_who.code_presentation.unique()))\nprint('studentAssessment.csv present in the archive although README says it is removed:', os.path.exists(os.path.join(DATA_DIR, 'studentAssessment.csv')))","metadata":{},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Train_Who            rows=  26,074  duplicated rows on key ['id_student', 'code_module', 'code_presentation']: 0\n","Test_Who             rows=   6,519  duplicated rows on key ['id_student', 'code_module', 'code_presentation']: 0\n","studentInfo          rows=  32,593  duplicated rows on key ['id_student', 'code_module', 'code_presentation']: 0\n","studentRegistration  rows=  32,593  duplicated rows on key ['id_student', 'code_module', 'code_presentation']: 0\n","courses              rows=      22  duplicated rows on key ['code_module', 'code_presentation']: 0\n","assessments          rows=     206  duplicated rows on key ['id_assessment']: 0\n","vle                  rows=   6,364  duplicated rows on key ['id_site']: 0\n","train/test overlap on key: 0\n","test students that also occur in train (other presentation): 17.9%\n","same presentations in train and test: True\n","studentAssessment.csv present in the archive although README says it is removed: True\n"]}],"execution_count":2},{"id":"af51493a","cell_type":"markdown","source":"| table | one row = | rows |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Train_Who` / `Test_Who` / `Train_Target_reg` | one student in one presentation (the prediction unit) | 26 074 / 6 519 / 26 074 |\n| `studentInfo`, `studentRegistration` | the same unit (demographics / registration date) | 32 593 |\n| `courses` | one module-presentation (length in days) | 22 |\n| `assessments` | one assessment of a module-presentation (type, due date, weight) – **no student info** | 206 |\n| `vle` | one learning resource (activity type) of a module-presentation | 6 364 |\n| `studentVle` | clicks of one student on one resource on one day (≈10.6 M rows, repeated rows per day exist) | ≈10.6 M |\n| `studentAssessment` | one submission by one student (**declared as removed by the organisers**, see section 4) | 173 912 |\n\n`studentInfo` / `studentRegistration` share the prediction grain (1:1). `studentVle` has *many rows per prediction unit* and must be aggregated to `(id_student, code_module, code_presentation)` **before** joining, otherwise the merge multiplies rows. `assessments` and `courses` are presentation-level (m:1 joins).\n\nThe split is **random across the same presentations** (not by time), so shuffled K-fold is a faithful local validation. About a fifth of test students also appear in train in another presentation; section 7.5 checks this does not make CV optimistic.","metadata":{}},{"id":"a6dd4940","cell_type":"code","source":"base = si.merge(sr, on=K, how='left', validate='1:1').merge(courses, on=P, how='left', validate='m:1')\nprint(f'studentInfo rows {len(si):,} -> base rows {len(base):,}  (must be equal)')\nassert len(base) == len(si) == len(train_who) + len(test_who)\n\nEDU = {'No Formal quals': 0, 'Lower Than A Level': 1, 'A Level or Equivalent': 2, 'HE Qualification': 3, 'Post Graduate Qualification': 4}\nAGE = {'0-35': 0, '35-55': 1, '55<=': 2}\nassert set(base.highest_education.unique()) <= set(EDU) and set(base.age_band.unique()) <= set(AGE)\nbase['highest_education'] = base.highest_education.map(EDU)\nbase['age_band'] = base.age_band.map(AGE)\nbase['imd_band'] = base.imd_band.str.extract(r'^(\\d+)')[0].astype(float) // 10      # 0..9, NaN kept\nPID = {m + p: i for i, (m, p) in enumerate(zip(courses.code_module, courses.code_presentation))}\ncourses['pid'] = np.arange(len(courses)); PLEN = courses.module_presentation_length.values   # PLEN[pid] = course length\nbase['pid'] = (base.code_module + base.code_presentation).map(PID).astype(int)\nCATS = {c: sorted(base[c].unique()) for c in ['gender', 'region', 'disability', 'code_module', 'code_presentation']}\ndef cast_cats(df):\n    df = df.copy()\n    for c, cats in CATS.items(): df[c] = pd.Categorical(df[c], categories=cats)\n    return df\n\ntrain = train_who.merge(y_tr, on='ID', how='left', validate='1:1').merge(base, on=K, how='left', validate='1:1')\nassert len(train) == len(train_who) and train.weighted_score.notna().all()\nprint('train rows:', len(train), '| missing values per column (only non-zero):')\nprint(train.isna().sum()[lambda s: s > 0].to_string())","metadata":{},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["studentInfo rows 32,593 -> base rows 32,593  (must be equal)\n","train rows: 26074 | missing values per column (only non-zero):\n","imd_band             887\n","date_registration     37\n"]}],"execution_count":3},{"id":"04182767","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 3. Exploratory analysis","metadata":{}},{"id":"99c2c8ba","cell_type":"code","source":"y0 = train.weighted_score\nprint(y0.describe().round(2).to_string())\nprint(f'exact zeros: {(y0==0).mean():.1%} | below 10: {(y0<10).mean():.1%} | below 20: {(y0<20).mean():.1%} | exactly 100: {(y0==100).mean():.3%}')\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(1, 3, figsize=(14, 3.8))\nsns.histplot(y0, bins=50, ax=ax[0]); ax[0].set_title('Target distribution')\nsns.boxplot(data=train, x='code_module', y='weighted_score', ax=ax[1], color='lightsteelblue'); ax[1].set_title('Target by module')\nsns.boxplot(data=train, x='code_presentation', y='weighted_score', ax=ax[2], color='lightsteelblue'); ax[2].set_title('Target by presentation')\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["count    26074.00\n","mean        52.50\n","std         29.72\n","min          0.00\n","25%         22.00\n","50%         60.50\n","75%         77.40\n","max        100.00\n","exact zeros: 1.3% | below 10: 14.4% | below 20: 23.6% | exactly 100: 0.008%\n"]},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"image/png":"","text/plain":["<Figure>"]},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":4},{"id":"d969c73c","cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Observation.** The target is strongly bimodal: mean 52.5, std 29.7, a block of near-zero scores (14.4 % of students are below 10, only 1.3 % exactly 0) and a main mass around 60–80. The distribution differs by module and presentation, so these identifiers matter.\n**Conclusion.** A constant prediction has MSE ≈ 883 (the variance). The distribution is far from Gaussian, which motivates the hurdle model (H5) and the check of errors by target range later.","metadata":{}},{"id":"3ab56b4a","cell_type":"code","source":"fig, ax = plt.subplots(1, 4, figsize=(15, 3.4), sharey=True)\nfor a, c in zip(ax, ['highest_education', 'imd_band', 'age_band', 'num_of_prev_attempts']):\n    g = train.groupby(c).weighted_score.agg(['mean', 'count']); g = g[g['count'] >= 30]\n    a.bar(g.index.astype(str), g['mean'], color='steelblue'); a.set_title(f'mean target by {c}'); a.tick_params(axis='x', labelsize=8)\nax[0].set_ylabel('mean weighted_score')\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()\nprint(train.groupby('code_module').date_registration.apply(lambda s: s.isna().mean()).round(4).sum(), '<- total share of missing registration dates (tiny)')\nprint('corr(target, date_registration) =', round(train[['weighted_score', 'date_registration']].corr().iloc[0, 1], 3))","metadata":{},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["0.0078 <- total share of missing registration dates (tiny)\n","corr(target, date_registration) = 0.001\n"]},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"image/png":"","text/plain":["<Figure>"]},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":5},{"id":"d02120f9","cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Observation.** Demographics show only weak monotone effects: higher prior education and a less deprived IMD band go with higher scores (Spearman about 0.11 and 0.10), more studied credits goes with slightly lower scores (−0.08). Registration date is practically uncorrelated with the target (0.001; only 0.8 % of dates are missing).\n**Conclusion.** Demographics are useful but cannot carry the model; the ladder in 7.1 confirms this. Behavioural data must carry the signal.","metadata":{}},{"id":"5118d730","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 4. Leakage and prediction-time discussion\n\n*Would this value be available at the moment when the prediction has to be made, and is it allowed by the task design?*\n\n| source | decision | reason |\n|---|---|---|\n| demographics, registration date, course length | **use** | known before / at the start of the course |\n| `studentVle` clicks (aggregated) | **use** | behavioural logs; they do not contain the outcome. They are known only as the course proceeds, so section 7.7 re-trains with information truncated at 25/50/75 % of the course to measure how much accuracy depends on \"end of course\" logs |\n| `assessments.csv` (type, due date, weight of each assessment of a presentation) | **use**, presentation level only | public course structure, contains no student information |\n| `studentAssessment.csv` (who submitted which assessment) | **do NOT use in the final model** | The README says it was removed on purpose (\"not a bug, but a feature\"); the target is the weighted sum of scores of exactly those submissions, so the *weight of submitted assessments* bounds the target from above. It is almost a re-encoding of the label. I quantify the leak in 7.6 but exclude it from the solution |\n| `date_unregistration`, `final_result`, `score` | not available | removed by the organisers |\n\nCohort-relative features (percentile of a student's clicks inside the same presentation) use **features only, never the target**, so they are not leaky.","metadata":{}},{"id":"847aa609","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 5. Feature engineering","metadata":{}},{"id":"26c66404","cell_type":"markdown","source":"Hypotheses behind the features:\n\n* **H1 – engagement volume**: students who click more, on more days / weeks, score higher.\n* **H2 – timing profile**: *when* a student is active (before start, each quarter) and for how long they stay (last active day, gap to the end) separates drop-outs from finishers.\n* **H3 – assessment participation proxy (my idea)**: a student who is active in the ~14 days before an assessment deadline probably works on that assessment. Without `studentAssessment` I can still approximate \"how many / how much weight of the assessments did the student engage with\" from the VLE logs and the public deadlines.\n* **H4 – cohorts differ**: engagement should be judged relative to the same presentation (percentile within the cohort).\n* **H5 – the target is a mixture**: a group of drop-outs near 0 and a group of finishers; a *hurdle* model (P(low) × mean(low) + P(high) × mean(high)) may beat a single regressor.","metadata":{}},{"id":"14daf5d4","cell_type":"code","source":"# ---- aggregate the 10.6 M-row click log once to (student, presentation, activity type, day) in chunks ----\nACT = sorted(vle.activity_type.unique()); ACT_ID = {a: i for i, a in enumerate(ACT)}; ACT_NAMES = ACT + ['unknown']\nsite2act = vle.set_index('id_site').activity_type.map(ACT_ID)\nCACHE = 'vle_daily.pkl'\ndef build_daily(path):\n    parts = []; n = 0\n    for ch in pd.read_csv(path, chunksize=2_000_000):\n        n += len(ch)\n        ch['pid'] = (ch.code_module + ch.code_presentation).map(PID).astype('int8')\n        ch['act'] = ch.id_site.map(site2act).fillna(len(ACT)).astype('int8')\n        parts.append(ch.groupby(['id_student', 'pid', 'act', 'date'], sort=False).sum_click.sum().reset_index())\n        tic(f'  chunk done, rows read so far {n:,}')\n    d = pd.concat(parts).groupby(['id_student', 'pid', 'act', 'date'], sort=False).sum_click.sum().reset_index()\n    d = d.astype({'id_student': 'int32', 'pid': 'int8', 'act': 'int8', 'date': 'int16', 'sum_click': 'int32'}).rename(columns={'sum_click': 'clk'})\n    return d, n\nif os.path.exists(CACHE): vd = pd.read_pickle(CACHE); n_raw = None\nelse:\n    vd, n_raw = build_daily(os.path.join(DATA_DIR, 'studentVle.csv')); vd.to_pickle(CACHE)\nprint(f'raw studentVle rows: {n_raw if n_raw else \"(cached)\"} -> aggregated (student, presentation, activity, day) rows: {len(vd):,}')\nprint('total clicks preserved:', f'{vd.clk.sum():,}', '| activity types:', len(ACT_NAMES))\ntic('vle aggregated')","metadata":{},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["[     3s]   chunk done, rows read so far 2,000,000\n","[     4s]   chunk done, rows read so far 4,000,000\n","[     5s]   chunk done, rows read so far 6,000,000\n","[     7s]   chunk done, rows read so far 8,000,000\n","[     8s]   chunk done, rows read so far 10,000,000\n","[     9s]   chunk done, rows read so far 10,655,280\n","raw studentVle rows: 10655280 -> aggregated (student, presentation, activity, day) rows: 5,404,406\n","total clicks preserved: 39,605,099 | activity types: 21\n","[    10s] vle aggregated\n"]}],"execution_count":6},{"id":"53e616a9","cell_type":"code","source":"# ---- assessment structure (presentation level only: contains no student information) ----\na = assessments.merge(courses[P + ['pid', 'module_presentation_length']], on=P, how='left', validate='m:1').rename(columns={'module_presentation_length': 'plen'})\na['due'] = a['date'].fillna(a['plen'])            # exams have no date -> end of the presentation\nprint('assessments rows', len(assessments), '-> with course info', len(a), '(must be equal);  assessments per type:', a.assessment_type.value_counts().to_dict())\ndef structure(cut=None):\n    s = a if cut is None else a[a.due <= cut * a.plen]\n    n = s.groupby('pid').agg(n_assess=('id_assessment', 'count'))\n    nd = s[s.assessment_type != 'Exam'].groupby('pid').agg(n_due=('id_assessment', 'count'), w_due=('weight', 'sum'), first_due=('due', 'min'))\n    t = s.pivot_table(index='pid', columns='assessment_type', values='weight', aggfunc='count', fill_value=0).reindex(columns=['TMA', 'CMA', 'Exam'], fill_value=0)\n    t.columns = ['n_TMA', 'n_CMA', 'has_exam']\n    r = pd.DataFrame(index=courses.pid).join(n).join(nd).join(t).fillna({'n_assess': 0, 'n_due': 0, 'w_due': 0, 'n_TMA': 0, 'n_CMA': 0, 'has_exam': 0})\n    return r.reset_index()\n\ndef vle_features(cut=None, detailed=True):\n    d = vd if cut is None else vd[vd.date.values <= cut * PLEN[vd.pid.values]]\n    daily = d.groupby(['id_student', 'pid', 'date'], sort=False).clk.sum().reset_index()\n    L = PLEN[daily.pid.values]\n    daily['phase'] = np.digitize(daily.date / L, [0, .25, .5, .75]).astype('int8')     # 0 = before start, 1..4 = quarters\n    daily['week'] = (daily.date // 7).astype('int16')\n    G = ['id_student', 'pid']\n    out = daily.groupby(G).agg(clicks=('clk', 'sum'), active_days=('date', 'size'), first_day=('date', 'min'), last_day=('date', 'max'), max_daily=('clk', 'max'))\n    out['active_weeks'] = daily.drop_duplicates(G + ['week']).groupby(G).size()\n    ph = daily.groupby(G + ['phase']).clk.sum().unstack(fill_value=0).reindex(columns=range(5), fill_value=0)\n    ph.columns = ['clk_pre', 'clk_q1', 'clk_q2', 'clk_q3', 'clk_q4']; out = out.join(ph)\n    ac = d.groupby(G + ['act']).clk.sum().unstack(fill_value=0)\n    out['n_act_types'] = (ac > 0).sum(1)\n    if detailed:\n        ac.columns = ['act_' + ACT_NAMES[c] for c in ac.columns]; out = out.join(ac)\n    # H3: clicks in the 14 days up to each non-exam deadline\n    asg = a[a.assessment_type != 'Exam']\n    if cut is not None: asg = asg[asg.due <= cut * asg.plen]\n    rows = []\n    for pid, grp in daily[['id_student', 'pid', 'date', 'clk']].groupby('pid'):\n        dt = grp.date.values\n        for _, r in asg[asg.pid == pid].iterrows():\n            m = (dt >= r.due - 14) & (dt <= r.due)\n            if m.any():\n                s = grp[m].groupby('id_student').clk.sum()\n                rows.append(pd.DataFrame({'id_student': s.index, 'pid': pid, 'w': r.weight, 'clk': s.values}))\n    W = pd.concat(rows).groupby(G).agg(dl_sum=('clk', 'sum'), dl_cnt=('clk', 'size'), dl_wsum=('w', 'sum'))\n    return out.join(W).reset_index()\n\ndef add_ratios(F, cut=None):\n    F = F.copy(); Lo = F.module_presentation_length * (1 if cut is None else cut)\n    F['clk_per_day'] = F.clicks / F.active_days.replace(0, np.nan)\n    F['active_frac'] = F.active_days / Lo\n    for q in ['pre', 'q1', 'q2', 'q3', 'q4']: F[f'share_{q}'] = F[f'clk_{q}'] / F.clicks.replace(0, np.nan)\n    F['trend'] = (F.clk_q3 + F.clk_q4) / (F.clk_q1 + F.clk_q2 + 1)\n    F['gap_to_end'] = Lo - F.last_day\n    F['dl_mean'] = F.dl_sum / F.n_due.replace(0, np.nan)\n    F['dl_frac'] = F.dl_cnt / F.n_due.replace(0, np.nan)          # share of deadlines with activity before them\n    F['dl_wfrac'] = F.dl_wsum / F.w_due.replace(0, np.nan)        # ... weighted by assessment weight\n    pr = F.groupby('pid')\n    for c, n in [('clicks', 'clicks_pct'), ('active_days', 'days_pct'), ('dl_sum', 'dl_pct'), ('last_day', 'last_pct')]:\n        F[n] = pr[c].rank(pct=True)                                # H4: relative to the same presentation (features only)\n    lc = np.log1p(F.clicks); F['clicks_z'] = (lc - lc.groupby(F.pid).transform('mean')) / lc.groupby(F.pid).transform('std')\n    return F\n\ndef feature_table(cut=None, detailed=True):\n    F = base.merge(vle_features(cut, detailed), on=['id_student', 'pid'], how='left', validate='1:1')\n    F = F.merge(structure(cut), on='pid', how='left', validate='m:1')\n    assert len(F) == len(base), 'a join changed the number of rows'\n    zero = [c for c in F.columns if c.startswith(('clk_', 'act_'))] + ['clicks', 'active_days', 'active_weeks', 'max_daily', 'n_act_types', 'dl_sum', 'dl_cnt', 'dl_wsum']\n    F[zero] = F[zero].fillna(0)\n    return add_ratios(F, cut)\n\nF_full = feature_table()\nprint('feature table rows:', len(F_full), '(= base rows), columns:', F_full.shape[1], '| students with no VLE activity at all: %.1f%%' % (100 * (F_full.clicks == 0).mean()))\ntic('features built')","metadata":{},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["assessments rows 206 -> with course info 206 (must be equal);  assessments per type: {'TMA': 106, 'CMA': 76, 'Exam': 24}\n","feature table rows: 32593 (= base rows), columns: 73 | students with no VLE activity at all: 10.3%\n","[    12s] features built\n"]}],"execution_count":7},{"id":"225fc942","cell_type":"code","source":"DEMO = ['gender', 'region', 'highest_education', 'age_band', 'imd_band', 'num_of_prev_attempts', 'studied_credits', 'disability',\n        'code_module', 'code_presentation', 'module_presentation_length']\nREG = ['date_registration']\nVLE_BASIC = ['clicks', 'active_days', 'active_weeks', 'first_day', 'last_day', 'max_daily', 'n_act_types']\nVLE_DETAIL = [f'clk_{q}' for q in ['pre', 'q1', 'q2', 'q3', 'q4']] + [c for c in F_full.columns if c.startswith('act_')]\nDEADLINE = ['dl_sum', 'dl_cnt', 'dl_wsum', 'dl_mean', 'dl_frac', 'dl_wfrac', 'n_assess', 'n_due', 'w_due', 'first_due', 'n_TMA', 'n_CMA', 'has_exam']\nRATIOS = ['clk_per_day', 'active_frac'] + [f'share_{q}' for q in ['pre', 'q1', 'q2', 'q3', 'q4']] + ['trend', 'gap_to_end', 'clicks_pct', 'days_pct', 'dl_pct', 'last_pct', 'clicks_z']\nALL = DEMO + REG + VLE_BASIC + VLE_DETAIL + DEADLINE + RATIOS\ndef get_xy(F, who, cols=ALL):\n    M = who.merge(F, on=K, how='left', validate='1:1'); assert len(M) == len(who)\n    M = cast_cats(M)\n    return M[cols], (M['weighted_score'] if 'weighted_score' in M else None)\nTR = train_who.merge(y_tr, on='ID', validate='1:1')\nX_all, y = get_xy(F_full, TR)\nX_test, _ = get_xy(F_full, test_who)\nprint(X_all.shape, X_test.shape, '| features per group:', {k: len(v) for k, v in dict(demo=DEMO, reg=REG, vle_basic=VLE_BASIC, vle_detail=VLE_DETAIL, deadline=DEADLINE, ratios=RATIOS).items()})","metadata":{},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["(26074, 71) (6519, 71) | features per group: {'demo': 11, 'reg': 1, 'vle_basic': 7, 'vle_detail': 25, 'deadline': 13, 'ratios': 14}\n"]}],"execution_count":8},{"id":"5fb55f8a","cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 5.1 EDA on the new features","metadata":{}},{"id":"85aa4993","cell_type":"code","source":"D = X_all.assign(y=y.values)\nzc = D.clicks == 0\nprint(f'students with zero VLE clicks: {zc.mean():.1%}; their mean target {D.y[zc].mean():.1f} vs {D.y[~zc].mean():.1f} for the rest')\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(1, 4, figsize=(17, 3.8))\nax[0].hexbin(np.log1p(D.clicks), D.y, gridsize=40, cmap='viridis', mincnt=1); ax[0].set_xlabel('log(1 + total clicks)'); ax[0].set_ylabel('weighted_score'); ax[0].set_title('Engagement vs target (H1)')\nsns.boxplot(x=pd.cut(D.clicks_pct, [0, .2, .4, .6, .8, 1.0], include_lowest=True), y=D.y, ax=ax[1], color='lightsteelblue')\nax[1].set_xlabel('click percentile inside presentation'); ax[1].set_title('Cohort-relative engagement (H4)'); ax[1].tick_params(axis='x', labelsize=7)\nsns.boxplot(x=pd.cut(D.last_day / D.module_presentation_length, [-1, .1, .3, .5, .7, .9, 5]), y=D.y, ax=ax[2], color='lightsteelblue')\nax[2].set_xlabel('last active day / course length'); ax[2].set_title('How long the student stays (H2)'); ax[2].tick_params(axis='x', labelsize=7)\nsns.boxplot(x=pd.cut(D.dl_wfrac, [-.01, 0, .25, .5, .75, 1.0]), y=D.y, ax=ax[3], color='lightsteelblue')\nax[3].set_xlabel('weighted share of deadlines with activity'); ax[3].set_title('Deadline-window activity (H3)'); ax[3].tick_params(axis='x', labelsize=7)\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()\nprint('Spearman with target:', D[['clicks', 'active_days', 'last_day', 'dl_sum', 'dl_wfrac', 'clicks_pct', 'studied_credits', 'imd_band', 'highest_education']].corrwith(D.y, method='spearman').round(3).to_dict())","metadata":{},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["students with zero VLE clicks: 10.4%; their mean target 52.2 vs 52.5 for the rest\n","Spearman with target: {'clicks': 0.392, 'active_days': 0.415, 'last_day': 0.549, 'dl_sum': 0.353, 'dl_wfrac': 0.45, 'clicks_pct': 0.403, 'studied_credits': -0.08, 'imd_band': 0.099, 'highest_education': 0.115}\n"]},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"image/png":"","text/plain":["<Figure>"]},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":9},{"id":"b2a36c60","cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Observation.** Engagement is clearly related to the target. *How long the student stays* (`last_day`, Spearman 0.55) is the strongest, then the weighted share of deadlines with activity (0.45), active days (0.42), the cohort click percentile (0.40) and raw clicks (0.39). Cohort-relative percentiles separate groups almost as well as raw clicks.\n**Surprise.** 10.4 % of students have **no VLE clicks at all**, yet their mean target (52.2) equals that of everyone else (52.5). So \"no logs\" does not mean \"dropped out\": for these students the click log is simply missing, and no engagement feature can say anything about them. This is the hardest group (see section 8).","metadata":{}},{"id":"dce12f38","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 6. Validation strategy","metadata":{}},{"id":"307165ba","cell_type":"markdown","source":"The test split is random across the same presentations, so I use **shuffled 5-fold CV** on the training set (fixed seed, **identical folds for all experiments**, so differences are comparable). Predictions are clipped to [0, 100]. I report MSE (competition metric) plus RMSE, MAE and R², and the std of fold MSEs as a noise yardstick: differences smaller than that are not conclusive. Section 7.5 compares with a student-grouped CV.","metadata":{}},{"id":"5f388d7b","cell_type":"code","source":"def cv_oof(make_model, X, y, groups=None, seed=SEED):\n    oof = np.zeros(len(X)); folds = []\n    kf = KFold(N_SPLITS, shuffle=True, random_state=seed) if groups is None else GroupKFold(N_SPLITS)\n    for tr, va in kf.split(X, y, groups):\n        m = make_model(); m.fit(X.iloc[tr], y.iloc[tr])\n        oof[va] = np.clip(m.predict(X.iloc[va]), 0, 100); folds.append(mean_squared_error(y.iloc[va], oof[va]))\n    return oof, folds\ndef metrics(y, p):\n    return dict(MSE=mean_squared_error(y, p), RMSE=mean_squared_error(y, p) ** .5, MAE=mean_absolute_error(y, p), R2=r2_score(y, p))\nHGB_BASE = dict(learning_rate=.06, max_leaf_nodes=31, min_samples_leaf=40, l2_regularization=1.0, max_iter=300)\ndef make_hgb(**over):\n    p = {**HGB_BASE, **over}; p['max_iter'] = max(20, int(p['max_iter'] * ITER_SCALE))\n    return lambda: HistGradientBoostingRegressor(categorical_features='from_dtype', early_stopping=False, random_state=SEED, **p)\noof_const, f_const = cv_oof(lambda: DummyRegressor(strategy='mean'), X_all[['studied_credits']], y)\nprint('constant-mean baseline:', {k: round(v, 1) for k, v in metrics(y, oof_const).items()})","metadata":{},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["constant-mean baseline: {'MSE': 883.5, 'RMSE': 29.7, 'MAE': 25.8, 'R2': -0.0}\n"]}],"execution_count":10},{"id":"5d8885b3","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 7. Experiments","metadata":{}},{"id":"5fbd368c","cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 7.1 Feature-group ladder (observation → hypothesis → change → metric)\nSame model (HistGradientBoosting, fixed parameters), one feature group added at a time.","metadata":{}},{"id":"444f263b","cell_type":"code","source":"rows = [dict(step='constant mean', n_feat=0, **metrics(y, oof_const), fold_std=np.std(f_const))]\ncols = []; LAD = {}\nfor name, g in [('+ demographics', DEMO), ('+ registration date', REG), ('+ VLE totals (H1)', VLE_BASIC), ('+ timing profile & activity types (H2)', VLE_DETAIL),\n                ('+ deadline-window & assessment structure (H3)', DEADLINE), ('+ ratios & cohort percentiles (H4)', RATIOS)]:\n    cols += g\n    oof, folds = cv_oof(make_hgb(), X_all[cols], y); LAD[name] = oof\n    rows.append(dict(step=name, n_feat=len(cols), **metrics(y, oof), fold_std=np.std(folds))); tic(name)\nladder = pd.DataFrame(rows).set_index('step')\ndisplay(ladder.round(2))\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 3.6)); ladder.MSE.iloc[::-1].plot.barh(ax=ax, color='steelblue')\nax.set_xlabel('5-fold CV MSE (lower is better)'); ax.set_title('Effect of each feature group'); plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["[    18s] + demographics\n","[    23s] + registration date\n","[    28s] + VLE totals (H1)\n","[    38s] + timing profile & activity types (H2)\n","[    50s] + deadline-window & assessment structure (H3)\n","[    65s] + ratios & cohort percentiles (H4)\n"]},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["                                               n_feat     MSE  ...    R2  fold_std\n","step                                                           ...                \n","constant mean                                       0  883.53  ... -0.00     10.62\n","+ demographics                                     11  820.64  ...  0.07      8.03\n","+ registration date                                12  817.52  ...  0.07      9.74\n","+ VLE totals (H1)                                  19  387.55  ...  0.56      7.05\n","+ timing profile & activity types (H2)             44  363.08  ...  0.59      6.48\n","+ deadline-window & assessment structure (H3)      57  362.01  ...  0.59      5.26\n","+ ratios & cohort percentiles (H4)                 71  362.30  ...  0.59      5.90\n","\n","[7 rows x 6 columns]"],"text/html":["<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n","  <thead>\n","    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n","      <th></th>\n","      <th>n_feat</th>\n","      <th>MSE</th>\n","      <th>RMSE</th>\n","      <th>MAE</th>\n","      <th>R2</th>\n","      <th>fold_std</th>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>step</th>\n","      <th></th>\n","      <th></th>\n","      <th></th>\n","      <th></th>\n","      <th></th>\n","      <th></th>\n","    </tr>\n","  </thead>\n","  <tbody>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>constant mean</th>\n","      <td>0</td>\n","      <td>883.53</td>\n","      <td>29.72</td>\n","      <td>25.82</td>\n","      <td>-0.00</td>\n","      <td>10.62</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>+ demographics</th>\n","      <td>11</td>\n","      <td>820.64</td>\n","      <td>28.65</td>\n","      <td>24.30</td>\n","      <td>0.07</td>\n","      <td>8.03</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>+ registration date</th>\n","      <td>12</td>\n","      <td>817.52</td>\n","      <td>28.59</td>\n","      <td>24.27</td>\n","      <td>0.07</td>\n","      <td>9.74</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>+ VLE totals (H1)</th>\n","      <td>19</td>\n","      <td>387.55</td>\n","      <td>19.69</td>\n","      <td>14.50</td>\n","      <td>0.56</td>\n","      <td>7.05</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>+ timing profile &amp; activity types (H2)</th>\n","      <td>44</td>\n","      <td>363.08</td>\n","      <td>19.05</td>\n","      <td>13.92</td>\n","      <td>0.59</td>\n","      <td>6.48</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>+ deadline-window &amp; assessment structure (H3)</th>\n","      <td>57</td>\n","      <td>362.01</td>\n","      <td>19.03</td>\n","      <td>13.90</td>\n","      <td>0.59</td>\n","      <td>5.26</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>+ ratios &amp; cohort percentiles (H4)</th>\n","      <td>71</td>\n","      <td>362.30</td>\n","      <td>19.03</td>\n","      <td>13.87</td>\n","      <td>0.59</td>\n","      <td>5.90</td>\n","    </tr>\n","  </tbody>\n","</table>"]},"metadata":{}},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"image/png":"","text/plain":["<Figure>"]},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":11},{"id":"d7958d73","cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Result (5-fold CV MSE, constant 883.5).**\n\n| step | MSE |\n|---|---|\n| + demographics | 820.6 |\n| + registration date | 817.5 |\n| + VLE totals (H1) | 387.6 |\n| + timing profile & activity types (H2) | 363.1 |\n| + deadline-window & structure (H3) | 362.0 |\n| + ratios & cohort percentiles (H4) | 362.3 |\n\n**Conclusions.** H1 is by far the biggest step (−430 MSE); demographics and registration give only about −66 together. H2 is a real gain (−24.5, well above the fold std ≈ 6.5): *when* a student is active matters beyond *how much*. H3 gives only −1.1 and H4 +0.3: both are **within noise**, so as standalone additions they are inconclusive/not useful for a tree model that already has the timing profile (`dl_wfrac` and `dl_pct` still appear in the importance ranking, so the information is partly redundant rather than absent). I keep them because they do not hurt, but I do not claim they help.","metadata":{}},{"id":"c293bffa","cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 7.2 Model comparison on the full feature set","metadata":{}},{"id":"c0ef4c85","cell_type":"code","source":"def make_ridge():\n    pre = ColumnTransformer([('num', Pipeline([('imp', SimpleImputer(strategy='median')), ('sc', StandardScaler())]), make_column_selector(dtype_exclude='category')),\n                             ('cat', OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown='ignore'), make_column_selector(dtype_include='category'))])\n    return Pipeline([('pre', pre), ('m', RidgeCV(alphas=np.logspace(-2, 3, 12)))])\ndef make_rf():\n    pre = ColumnTransformer([('num', SimpleImputer(strategy='median'), make_column_selector(dtype_exclude='category')),\n                             ('cat', OrdinalEncoder(handle_unknown='use_encoded_value', unknown_value=-1), make_column_selector(dtype_include='category'))])\n    return Pipeline([('pre', pre), ('m', RandomForestRegressor(RF_TREES, min_samples_leaf=5, max_features=.33, n_jobs=-1, random_state=SEED))])\nOOF = {'HistGradientBoosting': LAD['+ ratios & cohort percentiles (H4)']}\nres = [dict(model='HistGradientBoosting', **metrics(y, OOF['HistGradientBoosting']), fold_std=np.nan)]\nfor name, mk in [('Ridge (linear)', make_ridge), ('Random forest', make_rf)]:\n    OOF[name], folds = cv_oof(mk, X_all, y); res.append(dict(model=name, **metrics(y, OOF[name]), fold_std=np.std(folds))); tic(name)\ndisplay(pd.DataFrame(res).set_index('model').round(2))","metadata":{},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["[    67s] Ridge (linear)\n","[   143s] Random forest\n"]},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["                         MSE   RMSE    MAE    R2  fold_std\n","model                                                     \n","HistGradientBoosting  362.30  19.03  13.87  0.59       NaN\n","Ridge (linear)        447.05  21.14  16.27  0.49      7.76\n","Random forest         364.30  19.09  13.98  0.59      5.55"],"text/html":["<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n","  <thead>\n","    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n","      <th></th>\n","      <th>MSE</th>\n","      <th>RMSE</th>\n","      <th>MAE</th>\n","      <th>R2</th>\n","      <th>fold_std</th>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>model</th>\n","      <th></th>\n","      <th></th>\n","      <th></th>\n","      <th></th>\n","      <th></th>\n","    </tr>\n","  </thead>\n","  <tbody>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>HistGradientBoosting</th>\n","      <td>362.30</td>\n","      <td>19.03</td>\n","      <td>13.87</td>\n","      <td>0.59</td>\n","      <td>NaN</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>Ridge (linear)</th>\n","      <td>447.05</td>\n","      <td>21.14</td>\n","      <td>16.27</td>\n","      <td>0.49</td>\n","      <td>7.76</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>Random forest</th>\n","      <td>364.30</td>\n","      <td>19.09</td>\n","      <td>13.98</td>\n","      <td>0.59</td>\n","      <td>5.55</td>\n","    </tr>\n","  </tbody>\n","</table>"]},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":12},{"id":"3a7934f1","cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Result.** HistGradientBoosting 362.3, Random forest 364.3, Ridge 447.1 (R² 0.49 vs 0.59).\n**Conclusion.** Non-linear models beat the linear one by about 85 MSE: effects such as \"stays until the end\" are threshold-like and interact with the module. GBM and RF are statistically indistinguishable (difference 2 vs fold std ≈ 5.5–6), but they make different errors (MAE 13.98 vs 13.87 – comparable), which makes them useful blend members. Ridge is kept only as a diversity candidate.","metadata":{}},{"id":"3514741a","cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 7.3 Hypothesis H5 – hurdle (mixture) model\n*Observation:* the target is bimodal – a block of drop-outs near 0 and a block of finishers around 60–80.\n*Hypothesis:* a classifier for \"low outcome\" (target < 15) plus two separate regressors, combined as $p\\cdot\\mu_{low}+(1-p)\\cdot\\mu_{high}$ (which is the MSE-optimal prediction), can fit both regimes better than a single regressor.\n*Change:* `Hurdle` model, same HGB settings.","metadata":{}},{"id":"a0597a4c","cell_type":"code","source":"class Hurdle:\n    def __init__(self, thr=15, **p): self.thr, self.p = thr, p\n    def _hgb(self):\n        p = {**HGB_BASE, **self.p}; p['max_iter'] = max(20, int(p['max_iter'] * ITER_SCALE)); return p\n    def fit(self, X, y):\n        low = (y < self.thr).values\n        self.clf = HistGradientBoostingClassifier(categorical_features='from_dtype', early_stopping=False, random_state=SEED, **self._hgb()).fit(X, low)\n        self.lo = HistGradientBoostingRegressor(categorical_features='from_dtype', early_stopping=False, random_state=SEED, **self._hgb()).fit(X[low], y[low])\n        self.hi = HistGradientBoostingRegressor(categorical_features='from_dtype', early_stopping=False, random_state=SEED, **self._hgb()).fit(X[~low], y[~low])\n        return self\n    def predict(self, X):\n        p = self.clf.predict_proba(X)[:, 1]; return p * self.lo.predict(X) + (1 - p) * self.hi.predict(X)\nprint('share of training target below 15:', round((y < 15).mean(), 3))\nOOF['Hurdle HGB'], folds = cv_oof(lambda: Hurdle(), X_all, y)\nprint('hurdle:', {k: round(v, 2) for k, v in metrics(y, OOF['Hurdle HGB']).items()}, '| fold std', round(np.std(folds), 2))\nprint('single HGB:', {k: round(v, 2) for k, v in metrics(y, OOF['HistGradientBoosting']).items()})\ntic('hurdle')","metadata":{},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["share of training target below 15: 0.184\n","hurdle: {'MSE': 361.31, 'RMSE': 19.01, 'MAE': 13.77, 'R2': 0.59} | fold std 5.97\n","single HGB: {'MSE': 362.3, 'RMSE': 19.03, 'MAE': 13.87, 'R2': 0.59}\n","[   180s] hurdle\n"]}],"execution_count":13},{"id":"02e925ef","cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Result.** Hurdle 361.3 vs single HGB 362.3 (MAE 13.77 vs 13.87).\n**Conclusion.** H5 is only **weakly supported**: the gain of 1.0 MSE is far below the fold std (≈ 6), so on its own it is inconclusive. The mixture structure is mostly learned already by a boosted-tree regressor. The hurdle model is still the second most useful blend member because it gets a different error pattern.","metadata":{}},{"id":"95fb3857","cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 7.4 Hyper-parameter tuning (small grid, same folds)\nLarger learning-rate / leaf counts overfit with 26 k rows; I test shallower, more regularised settings.","metadata":{}},{"id":"3d911e5f","cell_type":"code","source":"GRID = [dict(learning_rate=.06, max_leaf_nodes=31, min_samples_leaf=40, l2_regularization=1., max_iter=300),\n        dict(learning_rate=.03, max_leaf_nodes=15, min_samples_leaf=60, l2_regularization=3., max_iter=500),\n        dict(learning_rate=.03, max_leaf_nodes=7, min_samples_leaf=80, l2_regularization=3., max_iter=700),\n        dict(learning_rate=.03, max_leaf_nodes=15, min_samples_leaf=100, l2_regularization=5., max_iter=500, max_features=.5)]\ntune = []\nfor i, g in enumerate(GRID):\n    o, f = cv_oof(make_hgb(**g), X_all, y); OOF[f'HGB cfg{i}'] = o\n    tune.append(dict(cfg=i, **g, MSE=mean_squared_error(y, o), fold_std=np.std(f))); tic(f'grid {i}')\ntune = pd.DataFrame(tune).sort_values('MSE'); display(tune.round(2))\nBEST = GRID[int(tune.cfg.iloc[0])]; OOF['HGB tuned'] = OOF[f'HGB cfg{int(tune.cfg.iloc[0])}']\nprint('best config:', BEST)","metadata":{},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["[   195s] grid 0\n","[   212s] grid 1\n","[   231s] grid 2\n","[   249s] grid 3\n","best config: {'learning_rate': 0.03, 'max_leaf_nodes': 15, 'min_samples_leaf': 100, 'l2_regularization': 5.0, 'max_iter': 500, 'max_features': 0.5}\n"]},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["   cfg  learning_rate  max_leaf_nodes  ...     MSE  fold_std  max_features\n","3    3           0.03              15  ...  359.29      6.22           0.5\n","1    1           0.03              15  ...  360.78      6.17           NaN\n","0    0           0.06              31  ...  362.30      5.90           NaN\n","2    2           0.03               7  ...  363.75      7.49           NaN\n","\n","[4 rows x 9 columns]"],"text/html":["<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n","  <thead>\n","    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n","      <th></th>\n","      <th>cfg</th>\n","      <th>learning_rate</th>\n","      <th>max_leaf_nodes</th>\n","      <th>min_samples_leaf</th>\n","      <th>l2_regularization</th>\n","      <th>max_iter</th>\n","      <th>MSE</th>\n","      <th>fold_std</th>\n","      <th>max_features</th>\n","    </tr>\n","  </thead>\n","  <tbody>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>3</th>\n","      <td>3</td>\n","      <td>0.03</td>\n","      <td>15</td>\n","      <td>100</td>\n","      <td>5.0</td>\n","      <td>500</td>\n","      <td>359.29</td>\n","      <td>6.22</td>\n","      <td>0.5</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>1</th>\n","      <td>1</td>\n","      <td>0.03</td>\n","      <td>15</td>\n","      <td>60</td>\n","      <td>3.0</td>\n","      <td>500</td>\n","      <td>360.78</td>\n","      <td>6.17</td>\n","      <td>NaN</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>0</th>\n","      <td>0</td>\n","      <td>0.06</td>\n","      <td>31</td>\n","      <td>40</td>\n","      <td>1.0</td>\n","      <td>300</td>\n","      <td>362.30</td>\n","      <td>5.90</td>\n","      <td>NaN</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>2</th>\n","      <td>2</td>\n","      <td>0.03</td>\n","      <td>7</td>\n","      <td>80</td>\n","      <td>3.0</td>\n","      <td>700</td>\n","      <td>363.75</td>\n","      <td>7.49</td>\n","      <td>NaN</td>\n","    </tr>\n","  </tbody>\n","</table>"]},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":14},{"id":"eb2c62b7","cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Result.** The best configuration (lr 0.03, 15 leaves, `min_samples_leaf` 100, L2 = 5, `max_features` 0.5) reaches 359.3 against 362.3 for the default (cfg 0); the shallowest (7 leaves) is worst at 363.8.\n**Conclusion.** More regularisation helps slightly (−3.0), but the whole grid spans only ~4.5 MSE, i.e. less than one fold std. Tuning is **not a major lever** here; the information in the features is the limit. Note the winner was picked on the same folds it is reported on, so 359.3 is slightly optimistic.","metadata":{}},{"id":"77f160b9","cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 7.5 Validation check – does a student repeated across presentations inflate CV?","metadata":{}},{"id":"2eb62552","cell_type":"code","source":"o_g, f_g = cv_oof(make_hgb(**BEST), X_all, y, groups=train_who.id_student.values)\nprint(f'shuffled K-fold MSE      : {mean_squared_error(y, OOF[\"HGB tuned\"]):.2f}')\nprint(f'grouped-by-student MSE   : {mean_squared_error(y, o_g):.2f}')","metadata":{},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["shuffled K-fold MSE      : 359.29\n","grouped-by-student MSE   : 360.04\n"]}],"execution_count":15},{"id":"dfd27ea1","cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Result.** Shuffled K-fold 359.3 vs grouped-by-student 360.0.\n**Conclusion.** Students that appear in several presentations do not inflate the CV (difference 0.7, noise level). The shuffled CV is a faithful proxy for the test split, which also contains ~18 % repeated students.","metadata":{}},{"id":"f886ae7a","cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 7.6 Quantifying the leak – what would `studentAssessment.csv` give? (diagnostic only, NOT used)\nTo justify my decision in section 4, I add the weight of submitted assessments (`w_sub`, `n_sub`) and look at the CV change. **These columns are not part of the final model.**","metadata":{}},{"id":"bbb780b6","cell_type":"code","source":"sa = rd('studentAssessment.csv')\nsa_w = sa.merge(assessments[['id_assessment', 'weight', 'code_module', 'code_presentation']], on='id_assessment', how='left', validate='m:1')\nassert len(sa_w) == len(sa) and sa_w.weight.notna().all()\nsg = sa_w.groupby(K).agg(n_sub=('id_assessment', 'count'), w_sub=('weight', 'sum')).reset_index()\nXl = TR[K].merge(sg, on=K, how='left', validate='1:1')[['n_sub', 'w_sub']].fillna(0)\nX_leak = pd.concat([X_all, Xl.set_index(X_all.index)], axis=1)\no_leak, f_leak = cv_oof(make_hgb(**BEST), X_leak, y)\nhs = X_leak.w_sub > 0\nprint(f'CV MSE leak-free: {mean_squared_error(y, OOF[\"HGB tuned\"]):.1f}  |  with submitted-assessment weight (leaky): {mean_squared_error(y, o_leak):.1f}')\nprint(f'largest y / w_sub * 100 among students with submissions: {(y[hs] / X_leak.w_sub[hs] * 100).max():.1f}  (target never exceeds the submitted weight)')\nprint(f'students without any submission row: {(~hs).mean():.1%}')\ndel sa, sa_w\ntic('leak diagnostic')","metadata":{},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["CV MSE leak-free: 359.3  |  with submitted-assessment weight (leaky): 265.4\n","largest y / w_sub * 100 among students with submissions: 100.0  (target never exceeds the submitted weight)\n","students without any submission row: 28.0%\n","[   285s] leak diagnostic\n"]}],"execution_count":16},{"id":"ef50c05c","cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Result.** Adding only the weight/number of submitted assessments (from `studentAssessment.csv`) drops CV MSE from 359.3 to **265.4** (−26 %). The target never exceeds the submitted weight (max ratio exactly 100), and 28 % of students have no submission row.\n**Conclusion.** This confirms the decision in section 4: this single file is worth ~94 MSE, much more than any engineered feature group after H1, and it re-encodes the label. The organisers state it was removed on purpose, so using it would undermine the purpose of the task; the final model **excludes it**. This number is a diagnostic of the leak, not a result of my model.","metadata":{}},{"id":"bfecbd12","cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 7.7 How much do we depend on end-of-course information? (timing check)\nEvery time-dependent feature is recomputed using only logs and deadlines up to a fraction of the course length.","metadata":{}},{"id":"cebfd28a","cell_type":"code","source":"ND = [c for c in ALL if not c.startswith('act_')]\nsweep = []\nfor cut in [0.25, 0.5, 0.75, None]:\n    Fc = F_full if cut is None else feature_table(cut, detailed=False)\n    Xc, _ = get_xy(Fc, TR, ND)\n    o, f = cv_oof(make_hgb(**BEST), Xc, y)\n    sweep.append(dict(cut='end' if cut is None else f'{int(cut*100)}%', MSE=mean_squared_error(y, o), RMSE=mean_squared_error(y, o) ** .5)); tic(f'cut {cut}')\nsweep = pd.DataFrame(sweep); display(sweep.round(2))\ndemo_reg = float(ladder.loc['+ registration date', 'MSE'])\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6.5, 3.5)); ax.plot(sweep.cut, sweep.MSE, 'o-', color='steelblue', label='model')\nax.axhline(demo_reg, ls='--', c='grey', label='demographics + registration only'); ax.axhline(y.var(), ls=':', c='r', label='constant mean')\nax.set_xlabel('share of the presentation observed'); ax.set_ylabel('CV MSE'); ax.legend(); ax.set_title('Accuracy vs. information available'); plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["[   299s] cut 0.25\n","[   314s] cut 0.5\n","[   329s] cut 0.75\n","[   341s] cut None\n"]},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["   cut     MSE   RMSE\n","0  25%  642.10  25.34\n","1  50%  486.47  22.06\n","2  75%  393.74  19.84\n","3  end  363.49  19.07"],"text/html":["<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n","  <thead>\n","    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n","      <th></th>\n","      <th>cut</th>\n","      <th>MSE</th>\n","      <th>RMSE</th>\n","    </tr>\n","  </thead>\n","  <tbody>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>0</th>\n","      <td>25%</td>\n","      <td>642.10</td>\n","      <td>25.34</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>1</th>\n","      <td>50%</td>\n","      <td>486.47</td>\n","      <td>22.06</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>2</th>\n","      <td>75%</td>\n","      <td>393.74</td>\n","      <td>19.84</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>3</th>\n","      <td>end</td>\n","      <td>363.49</td>\n","      <td>19.07</td>\n","    </tr>\n","  </tbody>\n","</table>"]},"metadata":{}},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"image/png":"","text/plain":["<Figure>"]},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":17},{"id":"bbfa50f2","cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Result.** MSE when only the first 25 / 50 / 75 % of the course is observed: 642 / 486 / 394, against 363 with the full course (constant mean 883; demographics only ≈ 818).\n**Conclusion.** The model depends strongly on late-course information: at 25 % the model achieves only about 46 % of the full variance reduction (883 → 642 vs 883 → 363), at 50 % about 76 %, and at 75 % about 94 %. The reported score is therefore valid for \"after the course\" (post-hoc estimation of the result), **not** for an early-warning system. Even so, the early model (642) is clearly better than demographics only (≈ 818), so early engagement already carries signal.","metadata":{}},{"id":"dad431ab","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 8. Model and error analysis","metadata":{}},{"id":"1bbc3ec7","cell_type":"code","source":"oof_best = OOF['HGB tuned']; resid = y.values - oof_best\nE = X_all.assign(y=y.values, pred=oof_best, resid=resid, sq=resid ** 2)\nprint('Total MSE:', round(E.sq.mean(), 1))\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(1, 3, figsize=(15, 4))\nax[0].hexbin(E.pred, E.y, gridsize=40, cmap='viridis', mincnt=1); ax[0].plot([0, 100], [0, 100], 'r--', lw=1)\nax[0].set_xlabel('out-of-fold prediction'); ax[0].set_ylabel('actual'); ax[0].set_title('Prediction vs actual')\ng = E.groupby('code_module', observed=True).sq.mean() ** .5; g.plot.bar(ax=ax[1], color='steelblue'); ax[1].set_title('RMSE by module'); ax[1].set_ylabel('RMSE')\nE['seg'] = np.where(E.clicks == 0, 'no VLE clicks', np.where(E.last_day < .5 * E.module_presentation_length, 'left before mid-course', 'active past mid-course'))\nsns.boxplot(data=E, x='seg', y='resid', ax=ax[2], color='lightsteelblue'); ax[2].set_title('Residual (actual - predicted)'); ax[2].set_xlabel(''); ax[2].tick_params(axis='x', labelsize=8)\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()\ndisplay(E.groupby('seg').agg(n=('sq', 'size'), mean_target=('y', 'mean'), MSE=('sq', 'mean'), share_of_total_sq_error=('sq', lambda s: s.sum() / E.sq.sum())).round(3))\nE['y_bin'] = pd.cut(E.y, [-1, 10, 30, 50, 70, 90, 100])\ndisplay(E.groupby('y_bin', observed=True).agg(n=('sq', 'size'), mean_resid=('resid', 'mean'), MSE=('sq', 'mean')).round(1))","metadata":{},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Total MSE: 359.3\n"]},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["                            n  mean_target      MSE  share_of_total_sq_error\n","seg                                                                         \n","active past mid-course  16440       63.367  192.625                    0.338\n","left before mid-course   6924       26.808  544.226                    0.402\n","no VLE clicks            2710       52.176  897.850                    0.260"],"text/html":["<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n","  <thead>\n","    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n","      <th></th>\n","      <th>n</th>\n","      <th>mean_target</th>\n","      <th>MSE</th>\n","      <th>share_of_total_sq_error</th>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>seg</th>\n","      <th></th>\n","      <th></th>\n","      <th></th>\n","      <th></th>\n","    </tr>\n","  </thead>\n","  <tbody>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>active past mid-course</th>\n","      <td>16440</td>\n","      <td>63.367</td>\n","      <td>192.625</td>\n","      <td>0.338</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>left before mid-course</th>\n","      <td>6924</td>\n","      <td>26.808</td>\n","      <td>544.226</td>\n","      <td>0.402</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>no VLE clicks</th>\n","      <td>2710</td>\n","      <td>52.176</td>\n","      <td>897.850</td>\n","      <td>0.260</td>\n","    </tr>\n","  </tbody>\n","</table>"]},"metadata":{}},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["              n  mean_resid    MSE\n","y_bin                             \n","(-1, 10]   3777       -17.4  608.0\n","(10, 30]   3505       -11.7  380.4\n","(30, 50]   2934        -9.0  275.7\n","(50, 70]   6249        -0.4  155.8\n","(70, 90]   8119        12.6  347.4\n","(90, 100]  1490        22.4  761.9"],"text/html":["<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n","  <thead>\n","    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n","      <th></th>\n","      <th>n</th>\n","      <th>mean_resid</th>\n","      <th>MSE</th>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>y_bin</th>\n","      <th></th>\n","      <th></th>\n","      <th></th>\n","    </tr>\n","  </thead>\n","  <tbody>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>(-1, 10]</th>\n","      <td>3777</td>\n","      <td>-17.4</td>\n","      <td>608.0</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>(10, 30]</th>\n","      <td>3505</td>\n","      <td>-11.7</td>\n","      <td>380.4</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>(30, 50]</th>\n","      <td>2934</td>\n","      <td>-9.0</td>\n","      <td>275.7</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>(50, 70]</th>\n","      <td>6249</td>\n","      <td>-0.4</td>\n","      <td>155.8</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>(70, 90]</th>\n","      <td>8119</td>\n","      <td>12.6</td>\n","      <td>347.4</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>(90, 100]</th>\n","      <td>1490</td>\n","      <td>22.4</td>\n","      <td>761.9</td>\n","    </tr>\n","  </tbody>\n","</table>"]},"metadata":{}},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"image/png":"","text/plain":["<Figure>"]},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":18},{"id":"9cab6f59","cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Observations.**\n* The model **shrinks towards the mean**: it over-predicts low scores (mean residual −17 for targets ≤10, −12 for 10–30) and under-predicts high scores (+13 for 70–90, +22 for 90–100). This is typical for MSE regression with noisy features, not a bug. The extremes have the largest MSE (608 and 762), the middle the smallest (156).\n* By segment: students with **no VLE clicks** have the worst MSE (898, 26 % of total squared error from 10 % of students; mean target 52, so nothing in the features can say whether they passed). Students who **left before mid-course** (MSE 544, 40 % of the error) are hard because they mix true drop-outs with students who did the assessments before leaving. Students active past mid-course are easy (193).\n* Module RMSEs are similar in magnitude, no module is a catastrophic outlier.\n\n**Next step if there were more time:** a better signal for \"no logs\" students (not available in this data) or per-deadline activity sequences for the early leavers.","metadata":{}},{"id":"e7eed412","cell_type":"code","source":"Xa, Xb, ya, yb = train_test_split(X_all, y, test_size=.2, random_state=SEED)\nmdl = make_hgb(**BEST)().fit(Xa, ya)\nbase_mse = mean_squared_error(yb, np.clip(mdl.predict(Xb), 0, 100)); rng = np.random.RandomState(SEED); imp = {}\nfor c in ALL:\n    d = []\n    for _ in range(3):\n        Xp = Xb.copy(); Xp[c] = Xp[c].sample(frac=1, random_state=rng.randint(1e9)).values\n        d.append(mean_squared_error(yb, np.clip(mdl.predict(Xp), 0, 100)) - base_mse)\n    imp[c] = np.mean(d)\nimp = pd.Series(imp).sort_values(ascending=False)\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7, 4.5)); imp.head(15).iloc[::-1].plot.barh(ax=ax, color='steelblue')\nax.set_xlabel('increase of hold-out MSE when the feature is shuffled'); ax.set_title('Permutation importance (top 15)'); plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()\nprint('hold-out MSE of this single model:', round(base_mse, 1)); print(imp.head(10).round(1).to_string())","metadata":{},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["hold-out MSE of this single model: 355.9\n","share_q4       76.3\n","last_day       46.0\n","code_module    30.1\n","dl_wfrac       13.7\n","trend           9.7\n","clk_q3          7.3\n","share_q3        6.9\n","clk_q4          6.1\n","act_forumng     5.9\n","dl_pct          5.6\n"]},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"image/png":"","text/plain":["<Figure>"]},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":19},{"id":"0b784f37","cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Permutation importance** (single model, 20 % hold-out MSE 355.9): the clear winners are `share_q4` (+76 MSE when shuffled), `last_day` (+46) and `code_module` (+30), then `dl_wfrac`, `trend` and the late-course clicks. In other words, the model essentially learns \"was the student still active in the last quarter, and in which module\". Demographics barely appear. Caveat: many of these features are correlated (shares, trends, last day), so importance is shared among them and individual values should not be over-interpreted.","metadata":{}},{"id":"b28393b4","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 9. Final model and submission","metadata":{}},{"id":"a8a55c09","cell_type":"code","source":"# blend of the model families; non-negative least-squares weights on out-of-fold predictions (no test information used)\nmembers = ['HGB tuned', 'Hurdle HGB', 'Random forest', 'Ridge (linear)']\nA = np.column_stack([OOF[m] for m in members]); w, _ = nnls(A, y.values); w = w / w.sum()\noof_blend = A @ w\nprint(dict(zip(members, w.round(3))))\nprint('blend OOF MSE:', round(mean_squared_error(y, oof_blend), 2), '| best single member:', round(min(mean_squared_error(y, OOF[m]) for m in members), 2))\n# the blend weights are fitted on the same OOF predictions, so check them with nested folds to avoid fooling myself\noof_nested = np.zeros(len(y))\nfor tr, va in KFold(N_SPLITS, shuffle=True, random_state=1).split(A):\n    wi, _ = nnls(A[tr], y.values[tr]); oof_nested[va] = A[va] @ (wi / wi.sum())\nprint('blend MSE with weights fitted out-of-sample (nested check):', round(mean_squared_error(y, oof_nested), 2))","metadata":{},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["{'HGB tuned': np.float64(0.422), 'Hurdle HGB': np.float64(0.353), 'Random forest': np.float64(0.225), 'Ridge (linear)': np.float64(0.0)}\n","blend OOF MSE: 356.65 | best single member: 359.29\n","blend MSE with weights fitted out-of-sample (nested check): 356.72\n"]}],"execution_count":20},{"id":"c606ed11","cell_type":"code","source":"final = {'HGB tuned': make_hgb(**BEST)().fit(X_all, y), 'Hurdle HGB': Hurdle().fit(X_all, y),\n         'Random forest': make_rf().fit(X_all, y), 'Ridge (linear)': make_ridge().fit(X_all, y)}\nP_test = np.column_stack([np.clip(final[m].predict(X_test), 0, 100) for m in members]) @ w\nsub = pd.DataFrame({'ID': test_who.ID.values, 'weighted_score': np.clip(P_test, 0, 100)})\nsub = sample_sub[['ID']].merge(sub, on='ID', how='left', validate='1:1')\nassert len(sub) == len(sample_sub) and sub.weighted_score.notna().all() and sub.ID.is_unique\nsub.to_csv('submission.csv', index=False)\nprint('written submission.csv', sub.shape); print(pd.DataFrame({'train target': y.describe(), 'test prediction': sub.weighted_score.describe()}).round(2).to_string())","metadata":{},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["written submission.csv (6519, 2)\n","       train target  test prediction\n","count      26074.00          6519.00\n","mean          52.50            52.60\n","std           29.72            22.88\n","min            0.00             1.95\n","25%           22.00            36.80\n","50%           60.50            56.82\n","75%           77.40            70.76\n","max          100.00            92.11\n"]}],"execution_count":21},{"id":"ecb027b7","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 10. Conclusions","metadata":{}},{"id":"3ef4ae67","cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Final result.** Local 5-fold CV MSE of the blend: **356.7** (RMSE 18.9) vs 883.5 for the constant mean (−60 %), R² ≈ 0.60. The nested check of the blend weights gives 356.7 as well, so the blending step does not fool itself. The final model is a non-negative blend of tuned HGB (0.42), hurdle HGB (0.35), random forest (0.23) and ridge (0, dropped automatically).\n\n**What actually worked.**\n1. Behavioural data from the VLE: −430 MSE for simple totals alone; timing profile and activity types another −24.5.\n2. Non-linear models over linear (−85).\n3. Blending: −2.6 vs the best single model, a small but consistent gain.\n\n**What did not work / was inconclusive.** Deadline-window features (H3, −1.1), cohort percentiles (H4, +0.3), the hurdle model (H5, −1.0 alone) and tuning (−3.0) are all within about one fold std, so I cannot claim they help individually; they are kept because they were not harmful.\n\n**Honest caveats.** (1) The accuracy is for \"end of the course\" information (7.7); it is not an early-warning model. (2) The excluded `studentAssessment.csv` would have given MSE ≈ 265 (7.6), a leak that I deliberately did not use. (3) A large part of the remaining error comes from students without VLE logs and early leavers (section 8), which these data cannot resolve. (4) Test predictions have a narrower spread (std 22.9) than the target (29.7), as expected for a shrinking regressor.","metadata":{}},{"id":"580477b5","cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Limitations / next steps.** (1) Without per-student assessment data, the model can only *infer* assessment participation from VLE activity; the ladder and the leak diagnostic show how large the remaining gap is. (2) Per-assessment features (activity around each individual deadline as separate columns) and sequence models over weekly click counts were not explored. (3) CV used one random split strategy; repeated K-fold would tighten confidence intervals on small MSE differences. (4) Blend weights come from the same OOF predictions they are evaluated on; the nested check in section 9 shows this optimism is small.","metadata":{}}]}